  abstract = {Multi-view Subspace Clustering (MSC) has gained significant attention due to its efficacy in capturing correlations among samples across multiple views. However, most existing MSC methods either rely on fixed dictionaries or use dimensionality reduction techniques to alleviate redundancy and noise, leading to the loss of structural information. In addition, they typically depend on weights and regularization terms to balance diverse views, inevitably introducing hyper-parameters that complicate optimization and affect clustering stability. To address these challenges, we propose a non-parametric multi-view subspace clustering (NpMSC) framework with projection-based adaptive dictionaries. Specifically, a variable low-dimensional projection matrix is employed to transform raw features into view-adaptive subspaces, generating dynamic dictionaries that adaptively reduce redundancy and noise while better preserving the structural information of the original data. Furthermore, to eliminate parameter dependence and achieve adaptive alignment across multiple views, the learned representation matrix is directly decomposed into view-specific clustering centroid matrices and a consensus clustering indicator matrix, ensuring stable and discriminative clustering results. As a result, redundancy reduction, representation learning, and consensus indicator construction are jointly modeled within a unified non-parametric framework. To efficiently solve the model, an effective optimization alg},
}

