  annotation = {中文辅助译名：DCAL：用于跨被试脑电图睡眠分析的双时间对比对抗性学习},
  abstract = {Accurate automated sleep analysis is essential for sleep health assessment, primarily comprising two key tasks: sleep staging and the detection of obstructive sleep apnea-hypopnea syndrome (OSAHS). However, cross-subject generalization remains a key challenge, as inter-individual electroencephalogram (EEG) variability introduces substantial feature shifts, while differences in sleep architecture and OSAHS event frequency give rise to label shifts. This paper proposes a Dual-Temporal Contrastive Adversarial Learning network (DCAL), a unified unsupervised domain adaptation framework designed to enhance the generalizability of both sleep staging and OSAHS detection using single-channel EEG. Specifically, DCAL holistically addresses the concomitant challenges of feature and label shifts. A Dual-contrastive Adversarial Learning (DAL) module is introduced to mitigate feature distribution discrepancies by synergizing temporal contrastive learning with an adversarial training paradigm, thereby learning robust, domain-invariant representations. Concurrently, a Learnable Re-Weighting (LRW) module is introduced to rectify the label distribution bias often induced by pseudo-labeling, proposing a novel mechanism that incorporates information gain to achieve cross-domain label consistency. Extensive experiments on a public dataset (UCDDB) and our newly collected OSAHS-PSG dataset validate the superiority of our proposed DCAL, demonstrating consistent performance improvements of 1%–7% acros},
}

